Executive summary

Autonomous construction on Mars requires robotic systems that perceive uncertain terrain, navigate hazards, transport materials, excavate resources, assemble structures, and maintain energy availability under severe environmental constraints. Accelerated computation may support these operations, but a faster benchmark does not automatically produce a safer, more energy-efficient, or more capable mission.

This document proposes a mission-level evaluation framework for computational architectures supporting the MARS-FIRST-BUILD robotic construction concept. It is a research programme, not a flight architecture.

The framework asks five questions in sequence:

1. Is computation a genuine bottleneck for a defined mission workload?
2. Does acceleration reduce total system energy within an explicit measurement boundary?
3. Does the accelerated architecture satisfy operational timing requirements, including the cost of whatever isolation mechanism is used to enforce them?
4. Can the architecture detect, contain, and recover from radiation-induced and hardware faults, including silent corruption that passes nominal checks?
5. Does any measured component improvement translate into a measurable mission benefit?

Six candidate execution classes are compared: an HPSC-class radiation-tolerant RISC-V reference, an optimized laboratory CPU, a CPU with conventional GPU acceleration, GPU graph/kernel-fusion execution, persistent/megakernel execution, and CPU with FPGA acceleration. Additional space-oriented processors may be added only when a specific candidate has been identified with documented capability and environmental-test evidence.

The framework includes explicit rejection criteria. A megakernel investigation may be terminated when both launch overhead and cross-decision redundancy are below defined thresholds. An acceleration claim fails when its isolation penalty consumes the timing benefit it was intended to deliver. A safety-relevant function may not rely on an unverified accelerator output regardless of benchmark speed. A component improvement is not a mission improvement unless the link is demonstrated.

All thresholds, gates, and hypotheses remain provisional. The pilot workload specified in Section 4.3 is intended to become the first experimentally actionable component of the programme.

