Abstract

This research proposes a mission-level evaluation framework for computational architectures supporting the MARS-FIRST-BUILD robotic construction concept. It compares an HPSC-class radiation-tolerant reference architecture, optimized laboratory CPU execution, conventional GPU acceleration, GPU kernel fusion and graph-based execution, persistent or megakernel approaches, and FPGA acceleration.

The central research question is not whether a processor or execution strategy can win a benchmark. It is whether a measured improvement survives the complete chain from workload execution to total energy, timing predictability (including the cost of timing isolation), fault containment (including silent corruption), environmental feasibility, and mission-level performance.

The framework separates five questions: whether computation is a genuine bottleneck; whether acceleration reduces total system energy within a defined boundary; whether timing behaviour satisfies operational constraints under interference and enforced isolation; whether radiation and hardware faults—including silent data corruption—can be detected and contained; and whether the improvement has measurable mission value. Explicit rejection criteria are defined.

This version introduces a fully specified pilot workload, a two-part megakernel screening rule, a timing-isolation budget, a fault-tolerance architecture classification, and a lexicographic decision procedure.

All thresholds and hypotheses remain provisional. No measurements have been conducted. This is a research programme, not a validated Mars flight architecture.

