1. Research problem

1.1 The optimization trap

Computational optimization frequently focuses on local performance metrics:

· Operations per second.
· Kernel execution time.
· Memory throughput.
· Latency per inference.
· Energy consumed by a processor during a benchmark.

These measurements can be useful, but they do not independently establish the value of an optimization within a complete robotic mission.

A faster terrain classifier may consume more energy because of accelerator activation, memory transfers, cooling requirements, or repeated execution. A lower-latency GPU workload may introduce unpredictable delays when it competes with other tasks, and enforcing predictable timing may itself consume a substantial fraction of the speedup [V]. A processor with excellent nominal performance may become unsuitable if faults cannot be detected, or if a silently corrupted output passes nominal validity checks while being wrong [V].

The proposed framework therefore treats computational performance as an intermediate variable rather than the final objective.

1.2 Research question

Under what measurable conditions does computational acceleration improve the safety, energy efficiency, reliability, and operational capability of a Mars surface construction mission?

This question is deliberately architecture-neutral. It does not assume that GPUs, CPUs, FPGAs, or any particular processor family is universally superior.

1.3 Secondary questions

· Which MARS-FIRST-BUILD workloads are genuinely computation-limited? [H]
· What proportion of end-to-end execution time is attributable to kernel launch, synchronization, data movement, and actual computation? [H]
· What proportion of that computation re-derives information that is unchanged or predictably evolving from previous cycles? [H]
· What proportion of total vehicle energy is attributable to computation? [H]
· Can accelerated workloads meet their timing requirements under interference, and at what isolation cost? [H]
· Can radiation-induced corruption, silent data corruption, hardware hangs, and incorrect outputs be detected before they compromise safety? [H]
· When is an HPSC-class radiation-tolerant processor sufficient, making GPU, FPGA, or megakernel complexity unnecessary? [H]
· What evidence is necessary before a computational improvement can be claimed to improve mission performance? [H]

