2. Parent mission architecture and design boundaries

MARS-AI-COMPUTE inherits its operational context from MARS-FIRST-BUILD.

The parent paper proposes four surface vehicles:

· R1 TERRAFORMER: Terrain preparation and excavation.
· R2 HAULER: Material transportation.
· R3 BUILDER: Construction and assembly operations.
· R4 INSPECTOR: Inspection, mapping, and verification.

The parent design also considers a fission-primary energy architecture with battery buffering, a nominal 10 kWe power-system target, and construction activities requiring geotechnical assessment.

These are inherited design assumptions, not demonstrated hardware capabilities.

The parent blueprint reports reference vehicle masses of 2,800 kg, 1,600 kg, 1,300 kg, and 280 kg, respectively [S][V]. Its estimated combined vehicle mass is approximately 5,980 kg, excluding other mission-system masses. Any system-level mass total, including power generation, deployment equipment, construction hardware, and supporting infrastructure, requires its own defined accounting boundary.

The 10 kWe figure is treated here as a design target for the proposed architecture, not as an established universal requirement for Mars missions. NASA's fission-surface-power development programme provides useful context, but a lunar power-system programme does not directly validate the proposed Mars architecture [S][V].

Boundary rule: MARS-AI-COMPUTE may optimize the allocation and use of computational resources. It may not compensate for unresolved propulsion, landing, thermal, radiation, geotechnical, power-generation, or structural-design problems.

