3. Hypotheses and falsification conditions

H1. Computation is a measurable operational bottleneck

Hypothesis [H]: At least one mission-relevant workload limits a vehicle's operational performance because its execution time, memory behaviour, or processing throughput is insufficient.

Required evidence [V]:

· A precisely defined workload (Section 4.2, Section 4.3).
· Representative input distributions.
· End-to-end timing measurements.
· CPU and memory utilization.
· Deadline or throughput requirements derived from the operational task.
· Evidence that computation, rather than sensing, communication, actuation, or physical movement, limits the task.

Falsification condition: If the task is predominantly limited by mechanical movement, sensor acquisition, communication, or another non-computational factor, and computational latency does not materially constrain the operation, acceleration is not justified for that task on performance grounds.

H2. Computational acceleration can reduce total vehicle energy

Hypothesis [H]: An accelerated implementation can reduce energy consumption for selected workloads without violating timing, reliability, thermal, or safety requirements—and this reduction is measurable relative to the HPSC-class reference, not only relative to a laboratory CPU.

Required evidence [V]:

· Energy measured over equivalent completed workloads.
· Identical or demonstrably equivalent output requirements.
· Inclusion of accelerator activation, memory transfers, idle power, synchronization, and relevant cooling overhead.
· A defined system boundary.
· Comparison against the HPSC-class reference (Section 5.1) and a properly optimized laboratory baseline, reported separately.

Falsification condition: If acceleration increases total energy, or its energy savings disappear when system overhead is included, or its advantage relative to HPSC-class silicon does not appear once radiation-tolerance overhead is included, the energy-efficiency claim is rejected for that workload.

H3. Faster execution can coexist with bounded operational timing

Hypothesis [H]: A selected computational architecture can satisfy task-specific timing requirements under defined levels of workload interference and fault conditions, including the penalty incurred by whatever mechanism is used to enforce the timing bound.

Required evidence [V]:

· Explicit deadlines and timing constraints.
· A documented scheduling and resource-isolation strategy.
· Measurements or defensible bounds on worst-case execution behaviour.
· Measurements of the performance penalty induced by the isolation mechanism (Section 8.4).
· Tests under concurrent workloads.
· Recovery timing after processor hangs, resets, or accelerator failure.

Falsification condition: If timing requirements cannot be demonstrated for the intended operating conditions, or if the isolation penalty consumes the majority of the acceleration benefit (Section 8.4), the architecture cannot be accepted for the affected safety-critical function.

H4. Algorithmic efficiency may outperform hardware-level optimization

Hypothesis [H]: For some mission workloads, reducing unnecessary computation, improving algorithms, or reducing the frequency of expensive operations may deliver greater mission benefit than changing the execution architecture.

Required evidence [V]:

· Equivalent task-quality requirements.
· Reproducible algorithmic baselines.
· Computational complexity and energy measurements.
· Evaluation under representative input conditions.
· Explicit treatment of accuracy, uncertainty, and failure cases.

Falsification condition (strengthened): If an algorithmically simpler method achieves the same defined output quality and mission-relevant timing within 10% of the energy of the more complex method, the complex method is rejected unless it provides a documented safety or reliability advantage that the simple method cannot. The 10% figure is a provisional project threshold, not a universal rule [H].

This hypothesis does not assume access to proprietary methods from any private AI-computing company. Open, reproducible techniques form the primary experimental basis.

H5. Mission-level benefit can be predicted from component-level improvements

Hypothesis [H]: Under a sufficiently defined system boundary, improvements in computational timing and energy can be translated into measurable changes in mission capability, power margin, operational availability, or task completion.

Required evidence [V]:

· A mission-level resource model.
· A traceable link between component measurements and operational outcomes.
· Sensitivity analysis.
· Uncertainty bounds.
· Evidence that the optimized component is relevant to the limiting mission process.

Falsification condition: If a component-level improvement cannot be linked to a measurable mission outcome, the component improvement may still be technically valid, but a mission-benefit claim is not justified.

