4. Workload definition before hardware selection

The research will not begin by choosing a GPU and searching for a task that makes it appear useful. That familiar engineering ritual is how benchmarks become marketing material.

Each workload must first be described independently of the hardware proposed to execute it.

4.1 Candidate workload classes

Terrain perception — Obstacle detection; terrain segmentation; rock and slope classification; hazard identification; mapping and localization.

Geotechnical assessment — Interpretation of subsurface or surface sensor data; terrain-property estimation; uncertainty estimation; identification of regions requiring additional inspection.

Construction planning — Route and worksite planning; material movement scheduling; placement and assembly sequencing; resource allocation among vehicles.

Inspection and verification — Detection of structural deviations; comparison between planned and observed geometry; identification of incomplete or damaged construction; assessment of whether an inspection result is sufficiently reliable for the next operation.

Energy and thermal management — Power-demand forecasting; battery and power-system scheduling; thermal-state estimation; selection among permitted operational modes.

These categories are candidate workloads, not evidence that any of them requires a GPU.

4.2 Workload characterization record

Each selected workload must have a record containing:

· Operational purpose.
· Input sources and data sizes.
· Output format and quality requirements.
· Required execution frequency.
· Deadline or acceptable latency.
· Expected and worst-case input complexity.
· CPU baseline.
· Baseline SWaP envelope (processor watts, total compute-subsystem watts, mass, thermal path). This is new in v0.4 and is mandatory.
· Memory and data-transfer requirements.
· Energy-measurement boundary.
· Failure consequences and recovery strategy.
· Cross-decision state semantics (what information persists or predictably evolves between consecutive invocations; Section 6.2).

No architecture comparison should proceed until the workload is sufficiently specified to support a meaningful baseline.

4.3 Fully specified pilot workload (new in v0.4)

The first pilot workload is defined as follows. It is chosen for mission relevance to R4 INSPECTOR and because it is concrete enough to measure.

Workload name: W1 — R4 INSPECTOR stereo obstacle detection.

