5. Phase 0: Hardware and environmental feasibility

Hardware feasibility precedes kernel optimization.

A theoretically fast architecture is irrelevant if its power demand, mass, thermal behaviour, radiation tolerance, software support, or qualification status makes it unsuitable for the intended vehicle.

5.1 Architectures to compare (revised)

A. HPSC-class RISC-V reference (mandatory baseline). A radiation-tolerant, multi-core RISC-V SoC of the class NASA is qualifying for long-duration missions. This is the mission-relevant reference. Every acceleration claim must be evaluated against it, not only against a laboratory CPU [S][V].

B. Optimized laboratory CPU (development reference). A well-optimized terrestrial implementation on a conventional processor. Useful for development and for detecting regressions. Not a mission baseline.

C. CPU with conventional GPU acceleration. Heterogeneous execution where selected workloads run on a GPU and control remains on the CPU.

D. CPU with GPU graph execution and kernel fusion. Reduces repeated launch or synchronization overhead using established optimization methods where the workload structure permits.

E. Persistent-kernel or megakernel execution. Considered only when measurements show both that launch overhead is significant and that cross-decision redundancy is substantial (Section 6.2–6.3).

F. CPU with FPGA acceleration. Candidate where deterministic dataflow, specialized processing, or bounded hardware behaviour could justify the implementation and verification effort.

G. Other space-oriented processors. Only when a specific candidate has been identified with documented capability, software support, and environmental-test evidence. The generic category "space-oriented processor" must not be treated as a single interchangeable option.

5.2 Qualification distinction

Commercial off-the-shelf hardware may be useful for research, development, or even selected operational roles. That does not establish its suitability for a Mars mission.

Similarly, research on commercial GPU radiation response, FPGA-assisted rover autonomy, or emerging high-performance space processors provides useful evidence, but none automatically qualifies the proposed system.

The study must distinguish:

· Performance demonstrated on a laboratory platform.
· Environmental testing under specified conditions.
· Radiation testing with a defined particle spectrum and fluence.
· Fault-tolerant behaviour demonstrated under controlled injection.
· Qualification for a particular flight environment.
· Operational suitability for the complete vehicle.

These categories are not interchangeable.

5.3 Software toolchain maturity (new in v0.4)

A space-qualified processor or accelerator is only useful if its compiler, libraries, debug tools, and runtime are mature enough for the intended team and mission schedule.

For each candidate architecture, record:

· Compiler and language support (including whether the target is a first-class or community-supported backend).
· Availability and licensing of numerical and perception libraries.
· Debugging, tracing, and profiling support on the target (not merely on a host simulator).
· Determinism and static-analysis tooling for the language and framework used.
· Track record of the toolchain on space missions or analogous deployments.
· Estimated person-months to bring a mission-ready build and verification toolchain into place.

A candidate with a strong benchmark result but an immature toolchain should be recorded as "not yet mission-viable" rather than as a winner [H].

