6. Phase 1: Establish the computational baseline

The first experiment, when the project reaches implementation, will establish how the unaccelerated workload behaves.

No megakernel, GPU, or FPGA advantage may be claimed before a credible baseline exists on both references defined in Section 5.1.

6.1 Required measurements

For each workload, measure or otherwise establish:

· End-to-end execution time.
· Execution-time distribution (not only the mean; include tail percentiles).
· CPU utilization.
· Memory consumption and bandwidth.
· Data-transfer time.
· Synchronization overhead.
· Cross-decision redundancy fraction (Section 6.2).
· Energy consumed per completed task, on both references.
· Output quality and task success rate.
· Deadline misses and failure behaviour.
· Baseline SWaP envelope (power, mass, thermal).

Measurements must be taken under documented hardware, software, thermal, and workload conditions.

A single average latency is insufficient when the application has deadlines or safety implications.

6.2 Launch, synchronization, and cross-decision redundancy

Define:

f_launch = (T_launch + T_sync) / T_end-to-end

where T_launch is the measured time attributable to relevant launch operations and T_sync is the measured synchronization contribution. The measurement method must avoid double-counting overlapping intervals.

Define, in addition:

f_redundancy = (C_total − C_essential) / C_total

where C_total is the total computation performed per decision cycle and C_essential is the computation that could not be re-used from the previous cycle under an ideal prior-state cache. Estimating C_essential requires instrumenting the pilot workload to distinguish re-derived work from genuinely new work [H].

Research on GPU megakernels and persistent execution has identified launch and synchronization overhead as important in some workloads, particularly certain machine-learning inference workloads [S][V]. Such evidence does not establish that the same overhead dominates Mars robotics workloads, and in many robotic perception pipelines the dominant redundant computation is not launch overhead but re-derivation of scene state that has not changed between frames [H].

6.3 Provisional screening rule (revised)

The megakernel investigation for a workload is terminated when both of the following hold:

1. f_launch < 5% (provisional project screening threshold), and
2. f_redundancy < 10% (provisional project screening threshold).

The rationale is that pipeline-level fusion and persistent execution require both meaningful overhead to eliminate and exploitable cross-decision state structure to exploit. If either is absent, the additional complexity has no measurable target.

Both thresholds are project screening rules, not universal laws of computing. The decision must account for measurement uncertainty (Section 6.5), variation across input classes, and whether the metrics change materially under a different operating mode.

A low launch-overhead fraction does not automatically rule out GPU acceleration. It only weakens the specific argument that reducing launch overhead justifies a megakernel.

6.4 Lower-complexity alternatives

Before developing a custom execution strategy, evaluate:

· Compiler optimization.
· Algorithmic simplification.
· Memory-layout improvements.
· Reduced unnecessary data movement.
· Kernel fusion.
· GPU graph execution.
· Batching where operationally permissible.
· CPU vectorization and optimized libraries.

Each option must be measured against the same workload definition, on both references where applicable.

The simplest implementation that meets the requirements is the preferred baseline unless evidence supports a more complex design.

6.5 Measurement uncertainty (new in v0.4)

Every reported measurement must be accompanied by:

· The number of repetitions and the aggregation method (mean, median, percentiles).
· A confidence interval or interquartile range.
· A note on measurement-timer resolution and operating-system jitter.
· A note on thermal state (warm-up time and any throttling observed).
· A note on whether outliers are included and, if excluded, why.

A screening decision made near a threshold (for example f_launch measured at 4% ± 3%) must be recorded as inconclusive rather than as a pass or fail.

No measurement may be entered into the evidence ledger (Section 18) without this metadata.

