7.2 Fair-comparison requirements

Each implementation must satisfy the same output-quality and task-completion requirements.

Comparisons must document:

· Processor and accelerator model.
· Clock and power settings.
· Software versions.
· Compiler settings.
· Memory configuration.
· Baseline SWaP envelope for each candidate.
· Input distribution.
· Warm-up and measurement procedure.
· Whether initialization and data transfers are included.
· Whether power measurements include the entire relevant system.
· Whether the timing bound was measured with the isolation mechanism enabled (Section 8.4).

A speedup measured only after excluding expensive setup operations, or only with the isolation mechanism disabled, must not be presented as end-to-end mission speedup.

7.3 Reporting rule

Report absolute values alongside relative improvements.

For example, a measured 2× speedup is incomplete without the original execution time, the resulting execution time, the energy cost, the isolation penalty, and the operational significance of the change.

A task reduced from 20 ms to 10 ms may be useful if a 15 ms deadline exists. The same improvement may have negligible mission value if the task is performed once per hour and does not constrain another operation.

