10. Phase 5: Radiation, silent corruption, and fault containment

Radiation exposure introduces failure mechanisms that must be considered independently of nominal performance. Potential concerns include transient computation errors, memory corruption, incorrect-but-plausible outputs, device hangs or resets, persistent hardware degradation, and failure of fault-detection mechanisms.

The actual likelihood and consequences depend on the selected hardware, shielding, environment, mission duration, and operating conditions. They cannot be inferred from the general statement that a processor is commercial or radiation-tolerant.

10.0 Fault-tolerance architecture classes (new in v0.4)

Every candidate must be classified by the fault-tolerance architecture it provides, not only by the mechanisms it lists:

· Class 1 — Detection only. Watchdog plus output validation (range, plausibility, temporal consistency). Suitable for Class C and Class D workloads (Section 8.1).
· Class 2 — Voting. Redundant execution of the same computation on independent execution units, with comparison or voting. Suitable for Class B workloads.
· Class 3 — Diverse redundancy. Independent implementations (different algorithms, different hardware, or both) with comparison or voting. Required for Class A workloads if any accelerator participates.

A candidate that cannot meet the required class for a workload fails the fault-containment gate (Section 14, C7–C8), regardless of its benchmark performance.

Voting between correlated failures is not redundancy. The verification design must state what independence means for the specific function and how its effectiveness will be evaluated.

10.1 Fault-injection programme

At the implementation stage, the study should investigate controlled injection of representative faults, including:

· Corrupted input data.
· Invalid or incomplete accelerator outputs.
· Memory errors.
· Timeout conditions.
· Interrupted execution.
· Device reset during a task.
· Disagreement between independent implementations.
· Failure of a validity check.
· Failure of the voting mechanism itself (new in v0.4).

The injection method must be appropriate to the hardware and must not be misrepresented as equivalent to a full radiation qualification test.

10.2 Silent data corruption

A particularly important failure class occurs when a computation returns an incorrect result without reporting an error.

For safety-relevant perception or planning, the system must consider whether incorrect outputs can be detected through:

· Range and consistency checks.
· Temporal consistency.
· Physical constraints.
· Independent sensor information.
· Diverse algorithmic implementations.
· Explicit uncertainty estimates.
· Safe rejection of invalid results.

No single mechanism is assumed sufficient. A structured failure-modes analysis (Section 10.5) must map each identified failure mode to the mechanism expected to detect it, with an estimated detection latency and residual risk.

10.3 Independent checking

Where the consequence of an incorrect result is significant, the architecture must provide an independent checking path of the class defined in Section 10.0.

The checker must differ sufficiently from the primary implementation to reduce shared failure modes where practical. Two implementations do not automatically provide reliable redundancy: they may share training data, assumptions, sensor failures, numerical limitations, or the same incorrect physical model.

Voting between correlated failures can create false confidence. The verification design must specify what independence means for the particular function and how its effectiveness will be evaluated [V].

10.4 Safe fallback

When an output is invalid, unavailable, or outside its verified operating conditions, the system should reject it and enter a predefined fallback behaviour.

Examples may include stopping motion, reducing speed, postponing excavation, requesting another inspection, or switching to a simpler verified procedure.

The appropriate fallback depends on the vehicle and task. It must be specified by the parent mission architecture and justified, rather than selected by an unconstrained AI planner at the moment of failure.

