11. Phase 6: Environmental and thermal assessment

Computational optimization cannot be separated from the environment in which the hardware operates.

The Mars surface introduces concerns including thermal cycling, dust exposure, mechanical vibration, restricted maintenance opportunities, and radiation. The actual severity depends on location, season, vehicle design, enclosure, and operating profile.

The study must investigate:

· The allowable operating-temperature range of each candidate.
· Heat generated under sustained and peak workloads.
· The path from the processor to the vehicle's thermal-control system.
· Changes in power consumption under different thermal conditions.
· Dust ingress and its possible effects on the complete computing enclosure.
· Thermal cycling and component-interface reliability.
· Performance degradation under representative environmental conditions.
· Recovery from overheating or environmental protection faults.

Laboratory thermal tests can support an engineering assessment, but they cannot by themselves demonstrate full Mars environmental qualification.

A processor that performs well on a laboratory bench may still be unsuitable for the complete vehicle.

11.1 Thermal test profile (new in v0.4)

At the implementation stage, the thermal programme must define:

· The number of thermal cycles and the temperature endpoints.
· Dwell times at each endpoint.
· The duty cycle of the workload during cycling.
· The thermal gradient across the compute enclosure.
· The pass criterion for performance and function at the end of cycling.

Without a defined profile, a "thermal assessment" is a narrative rather than evidence.

---

12. Phase 7: Algorithmic efficiency before hardware complexity

The research will explicitly separate two classes of improvement.

Algorithmic improvement reduces the amount of computation needed to satisfy a task. Examples include eliminating redundant inference, reducing unnecessary data processing, using an appropriate model size, improving search strategies, or avoiding repeated analysis of unchanged information.

Execution improvement changes how a given computational workload is executed. Examples include compiler optimization, parallel execution, kernel fusion, graph execution, or specialized hardware.

These categories can interact, but they must not be confused in reporting.

12.1 Required evaluation

Each proposed improvement must report:

· Baseline task performance.
· Resulting task performance.
· Output quality.
· Energy per completed task.
· Execution time.
· Failure modes.
· Implementation complexity.
· Verification burden.
· Cross-decision redundancy fraction (Section 6.2), if the improvement depends on exploiting prior state.

If a smaller or simpler algorithm achieves the same operational objective with lower energy and adequate reliability, it may be preferable to a faster but more complex accelerator architecture. The provisional threshold in H4 applies.

12.2 Open and reproducible evidence

The primary evaluation should use methods whose design and results can be inspected and reproduced.

Proprietary techniques may be discussed as motivation or future comparison targets, but undisclosed internal methods cannot serve as the sole basis for a scientific claim.

A research result is not independently reproducible merely because a company announces a speedup.

---

13. Phase 8: Mission-level value model

The final decision must combine performance, energy, reliability, and operational usefulness.

Define a mission-value assessment vector:

M = [T, E, R, S, V]

where:

· T: timing performance and deadline compliance (including isolation penalty).
· E: energy consumption within the defined system boundary (relative to HPSC-class reference).
· R: reliability and recovery behaviour.
· S: safety constraints and fault containment (including fault-tolerance class achieved).
· V: operational value to the mission.

This vector is not a universal scoring function. Its purpose is to prevent a single benchmark metric from silently replacing the mission objective.

13.1 Required decision sequence (operationalised, revised)

A candidate architecture must pass the following questions in lexicographic order. A failure at an earlier stage cannot be compensated by a later stage, regardless of score.

Stage 1 — Mandatory safety (S). Does the candidate achieve the fault-tolerance class required for the task category (Section 10.0), with the required detection latency and fallback behaviour demonstrated or credibly planned? If no, reject.

Stage 2 — Mandatory timing (T). Does the candidate meet the workload deadline under representative interference with the isolation mechanism enabled, and is the isolation penalty (Section 8.4) below the project's minimum-benefit threshold? If no, reject.

Stage 3 — Demonstrated advantage (T, E). Does the candidate show a measurable advantage over the HPSC-class reference in execution time, energy, or both, within stated uncertainty? If no, the candidate may still be recorded as a technical result but is not adopted.

Stage 4 — Energy and environment (E). Does the advantage survive full energy accounting (Section 9.4) and the environmental feasibility checks (Section 11)? If no, reject for mission use.

Stage 5 — Mission relevance (V). Is the advantage linked to a measurable mission outcome (Section 13.3, H5)? If no, record the technical result and defer adoption until relevance is demonstrated.

Stage 6 — Complexity (implementation cost). Is the improvement worth the documented complexity cost (Section 13.3) relative to simpler alternatives (Section 6.4)? If no, prefer the simpler alternative.

This ordering is itself a design decision, not a universal law. It must be documented and may be revised only with an explicit rationale entered in the decision log.

13.2 Complexity penalty

More complex architectures create additional engineering obligations, including software maintenance, interface management, debugging, verification, and recovery planning.

These costs should be documented rather than hidden behind a headline speedup.

