14. Validation gates (revised)

All gates remain open because no implementation or test evidence has yet been produced.

C1. Workload definition. At least one representative workload is specified with inputs, outputs, operational purpose, and acceptance criteria. Pilot workload W1 (Section 4.3) partially satisfies this gate; it remains open until reviewed by Roles B and D.

C2. Baseline credibility. An optimized baseline exists on both the HPSC-class reference and the laboratory reference, and its measurement procedure is reproducible. Open.

C3. Bottleneck identification. The limiting resource is supported by evidence rather than assumed, including launch overhead, synchronization, cross-decision redundancy, and non-computational limits. Open.

C4. End-to-end performance. Performance includes relevant initialization, data movement, synchronization, and output handling. Open.

C5. Energy accounting. Energy is measured or defensibly estimated within an explicit system boundary and compared against the HPSC-class reference. Open.

C6. Timing suitability. Required deadlines or timing bounds are demonstrated for the intended operational conditions, with the isolation mechanism enabled, and the isolation penalty is reported and below the minimum-benefit threshold. Open.

C7. Fault detection. Relevant failure modes and detection mechanisms are documented and evaluated, including silent data corruption and failure of the detection mechanism itself. Open.

C8. Recovery behaviour. The system's response to invalid outputs, hangs, resets, and interrupted execution is specified and tested at an appropriate development stage, and the achieved fault-tolerance class is recorded. Open.

C9. Environmental feasibility. Power, thermal, radiation, and mechanical requirements are assessed for the intended hardware and mission context, against a defined environmental test profile. Open.

C10. Independent verification. Safety-relevant outputs have a justified verification strategy, including explicit treatment of correlated failures, and the corresponding fault-tolerance class is achieved. Open.

C11. Mission-level relevance. A component-level improvement is linked to a defined operational benefit, and the link survives comparison against the HPSC-class reference. Open.

C12. Reproducibility and review. Methods, assumptions, measurement boundaries, uncertainty, and results are documented sufficiently for independent assessment, and measurement uncertainty is reported (Section 6.5). Open.

No gate may be marked closed merely because a document describes how it could be passed.

