15. SI TEAM collaboration and review responsibilities

SI TEAM is treated as a collaborative research group, not as a substitute for independent experimental evidence.

The proposed review structure is:

Role A — Systems architecture review. Check consistency with MARS-FIRST-BUILD, vehicle roles, power architecture, operational assumptions, and mission boundaries.

Role B — Computational performance review. Challenge workload definitions, baselines, profiling methods, comparisons, launch-overhead and redundancy measurements, and performance claims.

Role C — Energy and thermal review. Audit system boundaries, energy calculations, peak-power implications, heat generation, sensitivity analysis, and comparison against the HPSC-class reference.

Role D — Safety and fault review. Challenge failure assumptions, watchdog design, fault-tolerance class assignments, output validation, voting mechanisms, fallback behaviour, and common-mode failure risks.

Role E — Scientific falsification review. Search for counterexamples, alternative explanations, hidden assumptions, unsupported causal claims, and evidence that would invalidate the proposed hypotheses.

Role F — Editorial and provenance review. Verify citations, distinguish external evidence from inference, identify duplicated claims, maintain the evidence ledger, and audit the evidence-notation tags.

These are proposed responsibilities. They do not imply that every named AI system has independently completed a review or endorsed the present draft.

Any claim of independent agreement must be supported by actual separate analyses, documented disagreements, and a traceable resolution process.

