17. Limitations

This draft has substantial limitations.

First, it contains no original performance measurements. All workload-level performance claims remain hypotheses.

Second, the parent mission architecture is itself a paper design. Vehicle masses, energy requirements, and operational assumptions must be reconciled and validated before a computational architecture can be considered mission-ready.

Third, external studies often investigate workloads and environments different from Mars surface construction. Results from language-model inference, laboratory robotics, lunar power-system development, or terrestrial GPU systems cannot be transferred without an explicit applicability argument. The tiered ledger in Section 18 is intended to make this limitation visible but does not remove it.

Fourth, radiation tolerance, real-time behaviour, and fault recovery depend on the selected hardware and complete software architecture. General statements about GPUs, CPUs, FPGAs, or space processors cannot replace platform-specific evidence.

Fifth, the 5% launch-overhead and 10% cross-decision-redundancy thresholds are provisional screening rules. Their usefulness must be assessed against measurement uncertainty and the operational requirements of each workload.

Sixth, the timing-isolation budget (Section 8.4) is conceptually sound but has not been measured for any candidate. Its practical significance will only become clear once isolation mechanisms are implemented.

Seventh, the fault-tolerance classes (Section 10.0) are architectural categories, not verification results. A candidate cannot be assigned a class until the voting or redundancy mechanism has been tested.

Eighth, the mission-value framework is conceptual. The lexicographic ordering (Section 13.1) is a design decision, not a validated universal scoring rule, and the complexity-cost estimates (Section 13.3) are rough.

Ninth, the HPSC-class reference is treated as a design baseline because it is the mission-relevant class; specific configuration, performance, and qualification evidence must be obtained before any comparison against it is meaningful.

These limitations are not minor footnotes. They define the current boundary between the proposed research programme and demonstrated engineering results.

