19. Conclusion

MARS-AI-COMPUTE v0.4 establishes a falsifiable framework for deciding whether computational acceleration is justified in a Mars construction mission.

Its central principle is unchanged from v0.3: a faster computation is not necessarily a better mission system. v0.4 adds three safeguards that the external evidence showed were needed:

· The mission-relevant baseline is the HPSC-class radiation-tolerant reference, not a laboratory CPU. Every advantage must survive comparison against it.
· Timing claims must include the cost of enforcing the timing bound. An accelerator that is fast only when its timing is untrustworthy is not fast.
· Fault containment is classified, not merely listed. Safety-relevant functions require voting-level or diverse redundancy, not a watchdog alone.

Acceleration is justified only when it addresses a demonstrated bottleneck, improves a relevant operational metric, survives complete energy and timing analysis (including isolation penalty), and satisfies the required fault-containment class and verification conditions.

The framework deliberately permits negative results. A workload may not benefit from a GPU. A megakernel may offer no meaningful advantage once cross-decision redundancy is measured. A specialized accelerator may consume too much energy or impose excessive verification costs. An algorithmic simplification may outperform every hardware-level optimization.

These outcomes would not constitute research failure. They would eliminate unjustified complexity from a mission architecture whose resources, reliability, and ability to recover from failure are limited.

The immediate objective remains modest but technically important: take the pilot workload specified in Section 4.3, establish a credible baseline on both the HPSC-class reference and the laboratory reference, identify its actual bottlenecks (including cross-decision redundancy), and test whether an alternative architecture delivers a measurable improvement that survives isolation, energy, and fault-containment analysis.

Until that evidence exists, every claimed advantage remains a hypothesis.

Final design rule (unchanged). No acceleration claim without a baseline. No energy claim without a defined boundary. No timing claim without operational evidence and an isolation penalty. No safety claim without fault analysis and a declared fault-tolerance class. No mission-benefit claim based solely on a component benchmark.


