Appendix B — GPU Timing Isolation and WCET Evidence

The v0.4 timing-isolation budget (Section 8.4) is grounded in two literatures: (i) evidence that GPU timing variability under interference is large, and (ii) evidence that enforcing isolation has a measurable cost. Both are summarised here.

B.1 Timing variability under interference

The "On the Necessity of Real-Time Principles in GPU-Driven Autonomous Robots" paper demonstrates experimentally that when interfering workloads share an embedded GPU, a drone's reaction time degrades and collisions increase. The authors apply TimeWall, a component-based real-time framework, to isolate time-critical components and show that timeliness is preserved even under increasing interference .

Key quantitative framing from that work:

· The baseline implementation "breaks down and leads to many drone collisions" as interference increases, whereas the real-time-isolated implementation "does not lead to increased drone collisions" .
· The mechanism is scheduling-level: real-time locking protocols and priority assignment ensure bounded access to shared resources .

Implication for MARS-AI-COMPUTE: The presence of a GPU does not by itself make timing unbounded. The absence of an isolation mechanism does. v0.4's requirement that the timing bound be demonstrated with the isolation mechanism enabled is therefore the correct formulation .

B.2 WCET analysis on GPUs

The "Fat Block" paper (2026) quantifies why GPU WCET is difficult and what enforcement costs. Its findings:

· GPU kernels exhibit "highly variable and heavy-tailed execution-time distributions" due to "massive intra-SM concurrency, dynamic warp scheduling, and complex shared-resource interactions" .
· The proposed Fat Block approach enforces controlled execution semantics at the SM level: "resource-saturating thread blocks that intentionally occupy an SM exclusively, thereby eliminating inter-block interference while preserving representative kernel behavior" .
· Fat Block execution "significantly compresses execution-time distributions, reduces tail heaviness, and stabilizes high-quantile extrapolation" and achieves "substantially tighter pWCET bounds while maintaining statistical confidence" .

Implication for MARS-AI-COMPUTE: A credible GPU timing bound is achievable, but only by changing the execution model to enforce SM exclusivity. That change is exactly the kind of cost the isolation budget (Section 8.4) is designed to capture. A GPU that is fast only when SMs are oversubscribed with competing work cannot simultaneously be fast and bounded.

B.3 Isolation cost in the COOK access-control work

The COOK paper implements access control on an embedded Volta GPU "to ensure each GPU operation executes in isolation to reduce its timing variability" . Its own description acknowledges that "the strategies may cause some potential slowdowns for applications even in isolation" .

Implication for MARS-AI-COMPUTE: P_isolation (Section 8.4) is not a hypothetical. It is the quantified manifestation of a documented effect. The decision rule—"if the isolation penalty consumes the majority of the acceleration benefit, reject"—is therefore a safeguard against a predictable failure mode, not a speculative one.

B.4 Measurement protocol for P_isolation on W1

To measure P_isolation for pilot workload W1:

1. Implement W1 on the candidate architecture with the chosen isolation mechanism disabled. Record the execution-time distribution over ≥ 100 runs (Section 6.5). Call the median T_nominal.
2. Implement W1 with the isolation mechanism enabled. Record T_isolated over ≥ 100 runs under the same input distribution.
3. Co-run a representative interfering workload (e.g., a lower-priority mapping or logging task) in both configurations to ensure the comparison is made under realistic contention, not only in isolation .
4. Compute P_isolation = 1 − (T_isolated / T_nominal) with a bootstrap confidence interval.
5. Report P_isolation alongside the acceleration benefit over HPSC, not separately.

A candidate with a 3× nominal speedup over HPSC and P_isolation = 0.4 has an effective speedup of approximately 1.8×, and the decision must be made on the 1.8× figure.

