Appendix C — FPGA vs GPU Comparative Evidence: What the Numbers Actually Say

v0.4 treats FPGA acceleration (Candidate F) as a hypothesis, not a default. The comparative literature is more nuanced than a single headline number suggests.

C.1 Visual SLAM feature detection (arXiv:2510.13546)

This is the most directly relevant comparative study for perception workloads. Its findings:

· For non-learning-based detectors (FAST, Harris), "GPU implementations, and the GPU-accelerated V-SLAM can achieve better run-time performance and energy efficiency than the FAST and Harris FPGA implementations" .
· For a learning-based detector (SuperPoint), "its FPGA implementation can achieve better run-time performance and energy efficiency (up to 3.1× and 1.4× improvements, respectively) than the GPU implementation" .
· Accuracy: "the GPU-accelerated V-SLAM is more accurate than the FPGA-accelerated V-SLAM in general" .

Implication for MARS-AI-COMPUTE: The FPGA advantage is algorithm-dependent, not architecture-universal. A claim that "FPGA is more energy-efficient" without specifying the algorithm class is unsupported by this evidence. For W1 (classical stereo obstacle detection), the GPU may in fact be the more efficient choice; the FPGA case would need to be made on a learning-based or otherwise dataflow-friendly reformulation of the workload.

C.2 3D Gaussian Splatting for robotic training (IEEE, 2026)

An FPGA accelerator for 3D Gaussian Splatting reports "3.1× higher throughput and 9× better energy efficiency" compared with an NVIDIA Jetson AGX Xavier GPU baseline .

Caveat: The baseline is an embedded Jetson, not HPSC. The 9× figure cannot be transferred to a comparison against HPSC-class silicon without a separate argument (Section 9.4) [V].

C.3 FPGA Scene Memory for robots (LENS)

LENS "wins performance per watt over a Jetson Orin NX on every deployment task, by 1.9× to 5.7× at about a quarter of the power, and is faster in absolute latency on localization and Wayspots rendering" .

Caveat: Same baseline issue. Useful as evidence that FPGA can win on performance-per-watt for specific tasks, not as evidence that it will win against HPSC.

C.4 FPGA MPPI for sampling-based control (arXiv:2601.17231)

