Appendix D — Megakernel and Persistent Kernel Evidence: Applicability Audit

v0.4 replaces the 5% launch-overhead rule with a two-part rule (f_launch and f_redundancy; Section 6.2–6.3). This appendix audits the megakernel literature against that rule.

D.1 What the megakernel papers actually optimise

Paper Reported benefit Workload Mechanism
Mirage Persistent Kernel (MPK) "up to 1.7×" reduction in end-to-end inference latency  Multi-GPU LLM serving Compiler + runtime that transforms model inference into a single megakernel using SM-level graph representation and decentralized scheduling 
Event Tensor (ETC) "state-of-the-art LLM serving latency while significantly reducing system warmup overhead" Dynamic-shape LLM inference Unified compiler abstraction encoding dependencies between tiled tasks; handles shape and data-dependent dynamism
Ada-MK "up to 23.6% over vanilla TensorRT-LLM and 50.2% over vLLM" on an NVIDIA L20 LLM inference in online advertising DAG-based offline search that hoists runtime decisions to compile time; shared-memory constraint model
Fleet "1.3-1.5× lower decode latency than vLLM at batch sizes 1-8" on AMD MI350  Multi-die GPU LLM inference Persistent kernel execution and per-chiplet scheduling

D.2 Why these results do not automatically transfer to W1

1. The dominant workload is LLM token generation, where "every token generated during the decode phase triggers thousands of kernel launches" and "kernel launch overhead alone can account for 14.6% of end-to-end inference time" . This is precisely the regime in which the 5% launch-overhead screen would pass. W1 (stereo obstacle detection at 2 Hz) is not obviously in that regime [V].
2. The benefits depend on cross-call state semantics that the megakernel compilers exploit. Event Tensor explicitly targets "dynamic shapes and data-dependent computation" . A perception pipeline that processes independent frames has less exploitable cross-call structure unless the scene is temporally coherent—which is exactly what f_redundancy is designed to measure (Section 6.2).
3. The 14.6% figure is an LLM-specific measurement . It must not be cited as evidence that launch overhead is 14.6% of a rover perception workload.

D.3 Applying the two-part screen to W1

For W1, the screen proceeds as:

1. Instrument the baseline to measure T_launch + T_sync as a fraction of T_end-to-end (f_launch).
2. Instrument the baseline to estimate f_redundancy: what fraction of per-frame computation re-derives information available from the previous frame (camera pose, prior obstacle mask, unchanging terrain features).
3. If f_launch < 5% and f_redundancy < 10%, terminate the megakernel investigation for W1 (Section 6.3).
4. If either exceeds its threshold, proceed to a limited investigation, but only with a documented mechanism by which the megakernel would exploit the measured structure.

A megakernel paper's LLM speedup is Tier 3 evidence for W1: motivational only (Section 18).

