Appendix E — Fault-Tolerance Architecture Classes: Definitions and Test Criteria

v0.4 introduces three fault-tolerance classes (Section 10.0). This appendix defines them precisely enough to be assigned and tested.

E.1 Class definitions

Class Mechanism What it detects What it does not detect Suitable workload class
Class 1 — Detection only Watchdog + output validation (range, plausibility, temporal consistency) Hangs; grossly implausible outputs; some stuck-at conditions Silent data corruption that produces plausible but wrong output; correlated sensor faults C, D
Class 2 — Voting (homogeneous) Redundant execution of the same computation on independent execution units; comparison or majority vote Transient and permanent faults in one execution unit that produce divergent output Common-mode faults affecting all units (shared compiler, shared model, shared input) B
Class 3 — Diverse redundancy Independent implementations (different algorithms, different hardware, or both) with comparison or vote A broader class of design faults, including some shared-model failures if the diversity is genuine Faults common to both implementations (shared physical model, shared training data, shared sensor) A

E.2 Evidence that Class 2 is implementable on HPSC-class hardware

The JPL ARBITER work is the directly relevant evidence:

· ARBITER is "a Multi-Core Voting (MV) mechanism that performs real-time fault detection and correction across redundant cores" .
· It is "validated in both static optimization tasks (GFOLD) and dynamic closed-loop control (Attitude Control System)" .
· A fault-injection study "identifies the gradient computation stage in GFOLD as the most sensitive to bit-level errors, motivating selective protection strategies and vector-based output arbitration" .

Implication for MARS-AI-COMPUTE: Class 2 voting is not a paper concept. It has been demonstrated on next-generation multi-core space processors, including HPSC, with fault injection . The remaining question for any accelerator candidate is whether the accelerator's outputs can participate in a comparable voting scheme, or whether the accelerator must be treated as a Class 1 (detection-only) component regardless of its nominal capability.

E.3 Test criteria for class assignment

A candidate cannot be assigned a class until the following are demonstrated (Section 17, limitation 7):

Class Minimum demonstration Gate
Class 1 Watchdog detects a deliberately introduced hang within the stated detection latency; validation rejects a deliberately corrupted output C7
Class 2 Fault injection into one redundant unit produces a detected divergence and a corrected output in ≥ 99% of injected single-bit faults, with the residual rate characterised C7, C8, C10
Class 3 As Class 2, plus a documented argument for why the two implementations do not share the dominant failure mode, and a test that exercises the diversity (e.g., a fault that affects one algorithm but not the other) C10

The 99% figure is a provisional project threshold, not a universal requirement [H]. It must be justified against the severity of the failure consequence for the specific workload.

E.4 Correlated-failure test for Class 3

A diverse-redundancy implementation that shares a sensor, a training dataset, or a physical model does not provide the independence its class claims. The test must therefore include at least one injected fault in the shared element, to confirm that the diversity does not create false confidence. If the shared element's failure produces a common wrong output in both implementations, the class assignment is downgraded.

