Appendix F — NASA Rover Navigation Bottleneck Evidence: Annotated

v0.4's H1 depends on evidence that computation is a real bottleneck. This appendix annotates the NASA sources.

F.1 Direct statements of computational limitation

· NASA TechPort: "Existing methods of state estimation and route determination of planetary rovers are too expensive, both in computational time and power requirements. Many sensors and methods that are used in terrestrial systems are too large, too heavy, or too power hungry for space. Current optical methods used in rovers are slow due to limited computational power. As a result, they are limited in their speed and performance." 
· The same source notes that "small, high-cadence, minimalist rovers" face "even more crucial" computational constraints as SWaP budgets shrink .

F.2 Perseverance AutoNav specifics

· "MSL AutoNav typically performs 4 stereo computations per planning cycle" .
· "That very limiting computational constraint understandably led to design decisions that are less applicable to the VCE-powered M2020 rover. In particular ... we had a critical need for hazard assessment approach that would fit within the still very limited computational power of the RAD750" .
· Perseverance's enhanced navigation faced "strict safety and performance requirements in a highly uncertain environment only with a single-core CPU with extremely limited computing resources" .
· Stereo perception on Perseverance has exhibited "cases where stereo perception was significantly degraded, leading to multiple instances where autonomous navigation was no longer possible" .

F.3 What this evidence supports—and what it does not

Supported: Computation is a demonstrated bottleneck for rover navigation and stereo perception on past and current Mars hardware (H1 for navigation-class workloads) .

Not supported by this evidence alone:

· That computation is the bottleneck for inspection workloads on R4 (W1 is an inspection-class workload; the navigation evidence is analogous, not identical) [V].
· That the bottleneck on an HPSC-class processor would be of the same magnitude as on RAD750-class hardware. HPSC is reported to be ~500× faster than current radiation-hardened processors ; the bottleneck may shift from computation to sensing, actuation, or power [V].
· That the bottleneck is in the same pipeline stage (stereo matching vs. path planning vs. hazard assessment). The Perseverance evidence specifically identifies stereo and visual odometry as "the most computationally involved components of AutoNav" , but the relative cost on HPSC must be measured [V].

Implication for W1: The pilot workload is well chosen because it is the component that has historically been the bottleneck. But the experiment must measure whether it remains the bottleneck on HPSC, not assume it from RAD750-era evidence.

