Appendix I — Measurement Protocols for the Pilot Workload W1

This appendix specifies the measurements required by Sections 6.1–6.5 in enough detail to be executed.

I.1 Execution-time protocol

1. Warm-up: Run W1 for ≥ 60 s before measurement to reach thermal steady state (Section 6.5).
2. Repetitions: ≥ 100 completed frames for each configuration.
3. Input set: At least three input classes—benign terrain, low-contrast dust, partial occlusion (Section 4.3)—with ≥ 30 frames each.
4. Timing source: On-device timestamps where available; host-side timestamps must be corrected for transfer latency.
5. Reported statistics: median, 95th percentile, 99th percentile, maximum, and the number of deadline misses at the 400 ms deadline.
6. Uncertainty: bootstrap 95% confidence interval on the median and 99th percentile.
7. Thermal log: record package temperature at start and end of each block; if throttling occurs, the block is flagged.

I.2 Energy-per-task protocol

1. Boundary: compute subsystem only (processor + accelerator + memory + active cooling attributable to the compute subsystem), as defined in Section 9.2.
2. Measurement: power sampled at ≥ 1 kHz over each frame; energy integrated per completed frame.
3. Idle subtraction: measure idle power over a 5-minute no-work interval and report it separately; do not subtract it silently.
4. Isolation overhead: repeat with the isolation mechanism enabled (Section 8.4) and report both figures.
5. Reported statistics: mean and median energy per completed frame, with interquartile range.

I.3 Launch and synchronization measurement

1. Insert timestamps at the boundaries of each launch and synchronization event.
2. Sum only non-overlapping intervals (Section 6.2).
3. Report f_launch for each input class, not only the aggregate.
4. If the runtime provides launch tracing, cross-check the manual instrumentation against the trace.

I.4 Cross-decision redundancy estimation

1. Define the "prior-state cache" precisely: which quantities (camera pose, prior mask, static terrain features) could in principle be reused without recomputation.
2. Instrument the workload to count operations that read from the prior-state cache vs. recompute from raw input.
3. Report f_redundancy as a range across input classes, not a single number.
4. State explicitly that f_redundancy is an upper bound on exploitable redundancy: not all reusable computation is cheaply reusable.

I.5 Output-quality protocol

1. Ground truth: manual annotation or surveyed obstacle maps for each test frame.
2. Metrics: recall at the ≥ 0.3 m / ≤ 3 m specification, false-positive rate, and per-frame confidence calibration.
3. Report quality and timing and energy for each candidate—never one without the others (Section 7.3).

